00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:04.110
You'll also find a link in the description
below!

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.510
Long before Kim Jong-Un became the go-to tyrant
for five star absurdity, there was another

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.240
dictator on the block.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:14.940
Muammar al-Gaddafi ruled Libya for 42 years
with an iron fist wrapped in a sequined glove

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:18.170
so garish it would’ve put Liberace to shame.

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:21.410
His flamboyant reign was famous for its absurdity.

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:26.350
For every headline relating to his terrorist
activities, there were maybe a dozen more

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:31.170
about his plastic surgery addiction or all-female
bodyguard troupe.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:37.570
By the time Gaddafi was overthrown in 2011,
he was less feared than he was simply mocked.

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:42.470
But behind the monstrous vanity lay a man
who was simply a monster.

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:45.980
Rape in Gaddafi’s Libya was institutionalized.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.440
Mass executions were broadcast on TV.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.079
Dissidents were executed and their bodies
kept in freezers for the self-styled “King

00:00:53.079 --> 00:00:55.940
of All Kings” to visit at his leisure.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:00.860
But such depraved behavior wasn’t created
in a vacuum, it was only thanks to Libya’s

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:06.630
tortured history that Gaddafi was able to
imprint his psychosis on his nation.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:13.010
Today, we’re exploring how the life of the
“Mad Dog of the Middle East”.

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:23.799
Origins – the Boy and the Master

00:01:23.799 --> 00:01:29.580
Most histories of Gaddafi start at the obvious
place: the sweltering June day in 1942 when

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:34.320
he was born in a nomad’s tent near the dusty
fishing village of Sirte.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.450
But to really understand Gaddafi, you have
to go back further, all the way to September

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:40.780
28, 1911.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:44.960
That’s the day Italian warships appeared
off the coast of Tripoli.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.869
It was the beginning of the Italian occupation,
and the beginning of Libya’s very own reign

00:01:49.869 --> 00:01:50.880
of terror.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:56.549
For the next few decades, Libya was not a
nice place to be anything other than a pureblood

00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:57.549
Italian.

00:01:57.549 --> 00:02:01.770
Natives were worked to death in squalid labor
camps, while resistance fighters were brutally

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.170
executed in public.

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:06.750
The whole ghastly horror show only ended when
the Allies booted Italy from North Africa

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:08.259
in 1943.

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:12.810
By that point, over a third of all Libyans
had been murdered by their colonizers.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:18.050
If Gaddafi was cruel, it’s because he was
born into a world where cruelty was the only

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:19.050
currency.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.050
That being said, the boy himself missed the
worst of it.

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:24.680
With British help, King Idris of Cyrenaica
seized power in 1951, joining the three colonies

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:29.950
of Italian Libya into modern Libya and ushering
in a period of peace.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:31.760
Not that young Gaddafi had it easy.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.670
His family were poor Bedouins.

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:34.960
He lived in a tent.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.040
His only formal schooling was meant to be
learning the Quran.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.520
It’s only because he showed promise that
he was sent on to basic school.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.340
Presumably, his family hoped he was destined
for great things.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:48.470
Spoiler alert: that’s foreshadowing.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:52.790
While young Gaddafi was at school, scraping
his knees and carving his name onto desks,

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:56.280
Libya was sinking into a mire of poverty.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:00.640
King Idris was selling land to the US Army
in return for cash in his family pockets,

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:04.090
which was as popular as you’d expect.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:09.319
While this sort of naked corruption was humiliating
for ordinary Libyans, it was just a symptom

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:12.239
of the broader malaise gripping the Arab world.

00:03:12.239 --> 00:03:16.170
With culture in decline, and the rich looting
their countries, the average Arab was looking

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:20.660
for a champion, someone to make them feel
proud again.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:22.710
Enter Nasser.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:29.340
Colonel Gamal Abdel Nasser is a man who could
fill a dozen Biographics videos just by himself.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:34.599
The new ruler of Egypt following a bloodless
coup in 1952, he was the guy about to light

00:03:34.599 --> 00:03:39.769
the spark of Arab nationalism and send it
exploding across the region.

00:03:39.769 --> 00:03:43.019
In June 1956, he finally did.

00:03:43.019 --> 00:03:46.910
Furious with the British military presence
in his nation, and angry at the new state

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:51.610
of Israel on his borders, he nationalized
the British-owned Suez Canal.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:57.700
So the British joined forces with France and
Israel and invaded, triggering the Suez Crisis.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:02.599
The whole Suez Crisis is a beyond our scope
here, so let’s just skip ahead to the ending.

00:04:02.599 --> 00:04:06.069
Israel, France and Britain lost the Canal.

00:04:06.069 --> 00:04:11.870
Although their loss was more to do with the
US yelling at them “get out now, you idiots,

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:16.799
or you’re gonna spark WWIII!” that’s
certainly not how the Arab world saw it.

00:04:16.799 --> 00:04:19.229
Overnight, Nasser became a sensation.

00:04:19.229 --> 00:04:24.789
Combined with his working-class roots, poverty
reduction programs, and desire for Arab unity,

00:04:24.789 --> 00:04:27.759
he became a hero to millions.

00:04:27.759 --> 00:04:31.369
Among those newly inspired was teenage Gaddafi.

00:04:31.369 --> 00:04:36.309
Still in school, he watched the Suez Crisis
unfold with the sort of mixture of awe and

00:04:36.309 --> 00:04:39.990
excitement your parents felt watching the
moon landing.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:46.330
As with so many others, Nasser’s boldness,
his ambition lit a fire in Gaddafi’s soul.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:51.120
Before the Suez Crisis was over, he’d been
expelled from school for political agitation.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:56.240
But if you think a simple expulsion is gonna
halt Gaddafi’s new ambition to be the Libyan

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.099
Nasser, are you in for a surprise.

00:04:58.099 --> 00:05:03.490
The Libyan Nasser

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:10.069
Five years after his expulsion, young Gaddafi
managed to finagle his way into the military

00:05:10.069 --> 00:05:11.180
academy in Benghazi.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:15.779
He did so for the express reason of staging
a Nasserite coup.

00:05:15.779 --> 00:05:19.800
Seriously, he even brought a loyal group of
school friends along with him, all of whom

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:24.909
donned uniform for the first time on the understanding
that they’d soon be overthrowing the government.

00:05:24.909 --> 00:05:26.819
Albeit, not quite yet.

00:05:26.819 --> 00:05:32.490
For eight years, Gaddafi and his co-conspirators
did nothing more than slowly rise up the army

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:33.490
ranks.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:35.930
Gaddafi was made a captain and briefly trained
in the UK.

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:40.129
He met the American ambassador, who astutely
noted that the young soldier’s intelligence

00:05:40.129 --> 00:05:44.389
was only matched by his extraordinary emotional
instability.

00:05:44.389 --> 00:05:49.789
By 1963, Gaddafi had established a secret
Free Officers society in the army, modeled

00:05:49.789 --> 00:05:52.159
after Nasser’s old coup-planning team.

00:05:52.159 --> 00:05:56.929
By 1965, it had expanded its membership far
beyond his childhood friends, becoming the

00:05:56.929 --> 00:05:58.379
army club to join.

00:05:58.379 --> 00:06:03.779
Now convinced destiny was on his side, Gaddafi
simply needed a sign that Libya was ready

00:06:03.779 --> 00:06:05.110
for an uprising.

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:07.840
In 1967, he got it.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:12.759
On June 5, long-simmering tensions between
Israel and its Arab neighbors finally reached

00:06:12.759 --> 00:06:13.759
boiling point.

00:06:13.759 --> 00:06:19.039
The resulting Six Day War saw Israel wipe
the floor with Egypt, Syria, and Jordan while

00:06:19.039 --> 00:06:21.270
barely breaking sweat.

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:25.469
For our story though, it was the reaction
in Libya that mattered.

00:06:25.469 --> 00:06:29.300
Angered at such epic Arab humiliation, the
populace rioted.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:34.069
It was the first major sign of dissent against
Idris’s rule, until now Libyans had been

00:06:34.069 --> 00:06:36.749
almost uniquely undemonstrative in the Arab
world.

00:06:36.749 --> 00:06:40.960
For Gaddafi’s Free Officers, it was the
sign they’d been looking for.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:45.499
In August 1969, King Idris made the fateful
decision to go to Turkey for medical treatment.

00:06:45.499 --> 00:06:49.800
While he was out the country, the Free Officers
quietly seized control.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:53.520
When Libya awoke on September 1, it was to
a new regime.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:57.099
The monarchy had been toppled in a bloodless
coup, and the Free Officers now ruled via

00:06:57.099 --> 00:07:01.580
the Revolutionary Command Council, with Gaddafi
as its chair.

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:07.310
Aged just 27, the Bedouin boy born in a tent
became de-facto ruler of Libya.

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:13.129
So, given what we know about Gaddafi, how
was the coup received at the time?

00:07:13.129 --> 00:07:14.479
Surprisingly, rather well.

00:07:14.479 --> 00:07:16.020
Most Libyans embraced it.

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:19.580
While Idris had been popular with his clan,
most others felt his rule had been weak and

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:20.580
ineffective.

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:25.619
And Gaddafi wasn’t just being vain calling
himself the Libyan Nasser, ordinary Libyans

00:07:25.619 --> 00:07:30.560
really were crying out for a pan-Arab champion
to restore their pride and drag them into

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:31.800
the modern world.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:34.659
Even on the international scene, the reaction
was mostly warm.

00:07:34.659 --> 00:07:39.110
Henry Kissinger was willing to accept Gaddafi’s
insistence that the US abandon its Libyan

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:43.569
bases on the basis that the new leader was
at least an anti-Communist.

00:07:43.569 --> 00:07:47.699
In fact, the only group who turned out to
be unhappy with the idea of a Gaddafi-led

00:07:47.699 --> 00:07:52.120
coup were the Free Officers themselves, who
tried to stage yet another coup in December

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:55.020
to replace Gaddafi.

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:57.009
They were quickly crushed.

00:07:57.009 --> 00:08:02.800
As the sun dawned on the new decade, it was
on a Libya that was now totally under Gaddafi’s

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:03.800
control.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:08.589
The Master’s Death – the Wheels Fall Off

00:08:08.589 --> 00:08:15.879
The 1970s saw Libya get off to a fresh start
that was if not exactly good, then not utterly

00:08:15.879 --> 00:08:16.879
terrible.

00:08:16.879 --> 00:08:21.159
Following Nasser’s lead, the now Colonel
Gaddafi nationalized key assets like petroleum

00:08:21.159 --> 00:08:25.199
production, made education free, and distributed
money to the poor.

00:08:25.199 --> 00:08:29.379
Also like his hero, Gaddafi expelled the Jews,
along with all remaining descendants of the

00:08:29.379 --> 00:08:30.729
Italian colonists.

00:08:30.729 --> 00:08:35.829
It’s here that we get our first glimpse
of the man Gaddafi would become.

00:08:35.829 --> 00:08:40.599
For the new Libyan leader, it wasn’t enough
that the Italians simply left, they had to

00:08:40.599 --> 00:08:42.419
be humiliated.

00:08:42.419 --> 00:08:47.709
He forced all those going into exile to dig
up the corpses of their dead from the graveyards

00:08:47.709 --> 00:08:49.090
and take them with them.

00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:53.190
That very literal caravan of death must have
been a chilling sight.

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:55.800
However, this was all small potatoes.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:59.350
The outside world paid little attention to
Libya, even the Arab world.

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:03.000
At least, until September 28, 1970.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:07.120
That was the day that a massive heart attack
felled Gamal Abdel Nasser.

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:08.730
The master was gone.

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:13.470
It was a tough time for the Arab world, probably
made tougher by Gaddafi’s loud instance

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:17.019
that he was Nasser’s heir and would lead
them all from now on.

00:09:17.019 --> 00:09:21.380
While the Middle East mourned its hero, the
Libyan upstart began pulling all sorts of

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:25.800
tricks, from starting a war with Chad, to
getting thousands of Libyan youths to storm

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:30.569
the border with Egypt, waving petitions inked
in their own blood demanding the unification

00:09:30.569 --> 00:09:32.790
of Libya and Egypt.

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:37.079
Mad as this was, there was still a chance
at this stage that Gaddafi might wind up being,

00:09:37.079 --> 00:09:38.079
well, kinda OK.

00:09:38.079 --> 00:09:40.820
He’d made reforms, and nationalized petroleum
meant money to spend on bribing the mostly

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:45.690
nomadic populace with clean drinking water
and irrigation ditches.

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:51.680
Yet the clues to his future madness were still
there, if you knew where to look: inside Gaddafi’s

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:52.700
Green Book.

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:56.790
Released in 1974, the Green Book remains a
work like no other.

00:09:56.790 --> 00:10:02.670
An explanation of Gaddafi’s political philosophy,
it’s a heady mix of utopianism, Arab nationalism,

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:08.720
Islamism, socialism, tribal values, and a
great huge dollop of xenophobia, all thrown

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:11.290
together in a way that doesn’t really make
any sense.

00:10:11.290 --> 00:10:16.180
That may be why, when Gaddafi declared Libya
the Green Book’s prophesized utopian government

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:22.160
the Great Jamahiriyah in 1977, only Burkina
Faso ever seemed enthusiastic.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:24.839
The rest of the Arab world simply shrugged.

00:10:24.839 --> 00:10:26.550
Well, almost.

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:31.709
Gaddafi’s Green Book prophesized that other
Arab powers would be so jealous of the Jamahiriyah

00:10:31.709 --> 00:10:34.019
that they would try to destroy it.

00:10:34.019 --> 00:10:39.100
So, in July 1977, he attacked Egypt first
before they could attack him.

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:43.720
Only it turned out Egypt never intended to
attack, but now they were in this war they

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:45.750
were damned if they were gonna lose it.

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:50.779
500 dead later, the world had a very good
indication that Gaddafi’s Jamahiriyah did

00:10:50.779 --> 00:10:53.700
not intend to play nice.

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:59.069
The Mad Dog

00:10:59.069 --> 00:11:02.910
By the end of the 1970s, it was clear what
way the winds were blowing.

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:07.750
Frustrated by the end of his dreams of becoming
the next Nasser, Gaddafi instead settled for

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:11.449
becoming notorious on his own insane terms.

00:11:11.449 --> 00:11:17.779
Inside Libya, this meant a return to the repression
of the Italian era, only with a weirdness

00:11:17.779 --> 00:11:18.779
unique to Gaddafi.

00:11:18.779 --> 00:11:23.279
Cinemas, music concerts, and football matches
were all banned, followed by anywhere else

00:11:23.279 --> 00:11:25.589
where people could plot against the regime.

00:11:25.589 --> 00:11:30.029
Music was designated Western imperialism,
and a great bonfire of musical instruments

00:11:30.029 --> 00:11:32.560
burned in Tripoli’s central square.

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:37.079
On the economic front, rampant corruption
led to supermarkets so empty that there were

00:11:37.079 --> 00:11:39.220
frequent, deadly food riots.

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:41.209
Not that you could complain, of course.

00:11:41.209 --> 00:11:46.529
Complaining meant becoming another victim
in the mass executions broadcast day and night

00:11:46.529 --> 00:11:48.160
on public TV, another victim to be humiliated
then brutally slaughtered.

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:55.080
Gaddafi may have expelled the Italian colonizers,
but he kept the cruelest of their methods.

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:59.379
Outside Libya, this was also when Gaddafi
began throwing money at every extremist group

00:11:59.379 --> 00:12:00.379
on the planet.

00:12:00.379 --> 00:12:05.509
So it was Semtex for the IRA, weapons for
the Black Panthers, and money for the ANC.

00:12:05.509 --> 00:12:10.379
Yes, the same ANC led by Nelson Mandela, at
that point still sitting in prison.

00:12:10.379 --> 00:12:13.570
Remember that, ‘cause it’ll be important
later.

00:12:13.570 --> 00:12:17.720
As 1981 shuddered into view, Libya had basically
gone mad.

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:21.600
The death toll of the ongoing war in Chad
was brutalizing the whole society, now also

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:24.660
crumbling under a British-led economic embargo.

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:28.910
It was around this time that Gaddafi first
appeared on American screens, when US warships

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:32.499
were forced to shoot down two Libyan jets
over the Gulf of Sirte.

00:12:32.499 --> 00:12:38.079
It was the first military clash between America
and an Arab power in modern times.

00:12:38.079 --> 00:12:41.600
As you already know, it wouldn’t be the
last.

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:44.629
Fast becoming an international pariah, Gaddafi
doubled down.

00:12:44.629 --> 00:12:49.240
In the early 1980s, he made a public speech
in which he called Libyans living abroad “stray

00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:51.550
dogs” who would be hunted down.

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:55.940
He also promised to bring chaos to Europe,
a promise he quickly fulfilled.

00:12:55.940 --> 00:13:00.580
It would take the rest of our time today to
give you a running timeline of Gaddafi’s

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:02.499
atrocities over the next few years.

00:13:02.499 --> 00:13:05.630
Suffice to say they were numerous and shocking.

00:13:05.630 --> 00:13:09.749
A highlights, or maybe a lowlights reel might
go something like this:

00:13:09.749 --> 00:13:10.749
April 1984.

00:13:10.749 --> 00:13:15.540
A submachinegun fires out the window of the
Libyan embassy in London, injuring 11 anti-Gaddafi

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:19.490
protestors and killing 25 year old policewoman
Yvonne Fletcher.

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:20.990
December 1985.

00:13:20.990 --> 00:13:26.490
Arab terrorists funded by Libya launch simultaneous
gun attacks on Rome and Vienna airports, killing

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:28.939
19 and wounding 138.

00:13:28.939 --> 00:13:31.700
Their targets were Jewish passengers.

00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:33.220
April 1986.

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:36.620
Libyan intelligence bombs a West Berlin nightclub
frequented by US soldiers.

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:42.720
2 soldiers and a Turkish civilian die, while
nearly 230 are injured.

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:46.490
It was this last one that finally made the
international community take action.

00:13:46.490 --> 00:13:50.850
Faced with dead American soldiers, Ronald
Reagan authorized a bombing run on Tripoli,

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:53.199
intended to take out Gaddafi himself.

00:13:53.199 --> 00:13:57.999
Just days after the bomb went off in Berlin,
American jets streaked across the pale blue

00:13:57.999 --> 00:14:01.230
Libyan skies, raining missiles onto the capital.

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:06.499
In his compound, Mad Dog Gaddafi was shaken
to life by an enormous explosion.

00:14:06.499 --> 00:14:11.070
Staggering outside, he supposedly found the
bomb crater that had been meant for him.

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:16.079
Near its smoking remains lay the torn and
bloodied body of his adopted daughter.

00:14:16.079 --> 00:14:19.740
The American bombs may have failed in their
goal of killing the dictator, but they at

00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:22.759
least seemed to shake him to his senses.

00:14:22.759 --> 00:14:24.470
Attacks on Europe faltered.

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:27.440
In 1987, Gaddafi officially ended the war
in Chad.

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:32.730
In 1988, he even mimicked Gorbachev’s Perestroika
reforms in the Soviet Union, allowing capitalism

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:37.819
back into Libya and personally standing atop
a bulldozer that smashed down the wall of

00:14:37.819 --> 00:14:40.740
a jail in Tripoli, freeing hundreds of political
prisoners.

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:45.500
At the time, it looked like a cynical ploy
for winning back the hearts of a population

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:47.110
he’d abused for so long.

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:51.329
Now we know the Mad Dog was likely just playing
for time.

00:14:51.329 --> 00:14:57.139
Shortly after 6pm on December 21 that year,
Pan Am Flight 103 took off from Heathrow Airport

00:14:57.139 --> 00:14:58.139
in London.

00:14:58.139 --> 00:15:01.529
It flew upwards, over Britain, until it reached
the skies above the village of Lockerbie,

00:15:01.529 --> 00:15:02.529
Scotland.

00:15:02.529 --> 00:15:04.490
There, it disappeared from radar screens.

00:15:04.490 --> 00:15:07.829
When a control tower tried to signal it, only
silence returned.

00:15:07.829 --> 00:15:09.819
We all know what had happened.

00:15:09.819 --> 00:15:16.339
At two minutes past seven, a Libyan bomb exploded
in the hold of Pan Am 103, killing all 259

00:15:16.339 --> 00:15:17.339
onboard.

00:15:17.339 --> 00:15:22.020
Flaming wreckage then crashed down onto Lockerbie,
killing a further 11 people on the ground.

00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:25.950
In the aftermath of the bombing, it took an
international investigation two years to pin

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:27.220
the blame on Libya.

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:31.149
By that time, Gaddafi’s agents had already
downed another aircraft, the French operated

00:15:31.149 --> 00:15:37.720
UTA Flight 772, which exploded over Niger
in 1989, killing 170.

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:41.860
In 1992, the UN instigated a harsh sanctions
regime against Tripoli.

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:43.819
It was the beginning of Gaddafi’s total
isolation from the world.

00:15:43.819 --> 00:15:45.889
Now just before we get into the effects of
that isolation on the country, I do want to

00:15:45.889 --> 00:15:47.379
take a moment to thank the people who make
this video possible - Brilliant.

00:15:47.379 --> 00:15:49.319
And I know mid-roll ads are not great for
the story here, but these videos are long

00:15:49.319 --> 00:15:50.809
so I hope you appreciate our need to have
sponsors make them possible.

00:15:50.809 --> 00:15:52.189
Brilliant are a learning platform which teaches
through something called active learning.

00:15:52.189 --> 00:15:53.886
I don't know if you've ever had to sit down
with a science textbook, but I have, and there

00:15:53.886 --> 00:15:54.886
sure was a lot of "Wait, what?" and re-reading
pages 6 times and still not understanding

00:15:54.886 --> 00:15:55.886
what's going on.

00:15:55.886 --> 00:15:56.886
That's passive learning, and it's a slow way
to go.

00:15:56.886 --> 00:15:57.886
There is nothing there to test you right away
so your brain sort of glosses over the information...

00:15:57.886 --> 00:15:58.886
Again, and again...

00:15:58.886 --> 00:15:59.886
Brilliant solves that problem with active
