00:16:07.886 --> 00:16:08.886
Support biographics by going to brilliant.org/biographics.

00:16:08.886 --> 00:16:09.886
And now let's get back to Gadaffi and his
isolation problem.

00:16:09.886 --> 00:16:10.886
The ‘90s – Isolation

00:16:10.886 --> 00:16:11.886
If Gaddafi had begun 1988 trying to emulate
the reformist zeal of Gorbachev, he entered

00:16:11.886 --> 00:16:12.886
the 1990s an isolated madman, no longer caring
what the world thought.

00:16:12.886 --> 00:16:13.886
It’s around this time that some of the darkest
characteristics of the Mad Dog began to emerge.

00:16:13.886 --> 00:16:14.886
There are tales of secret, soundproofed chambers
built into public buildings across Tripoli.

00:16:14.886 --> 00:16:16.709
If Gaddafi happened to be visiting and saw
a girl he liked, she would be slipped away

00:16:16.709 --> 00:16:20.149
and locked in these rooms until he was ready
to come and rape her.

00:16:20.149 --> 00:16:23.160
Those who disappointed him were never heard
from again.

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:26.040
Those he liked might become one of his all-female
bodyguard retinue.

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:28.079
Then there are the young boys.

00:16:28.079 --> 00:16:30.669
They were all underage, all pretty, in their
own way.

00:16:30.669 --> 00:16:34.369
Kept caged beneath his compound, they formed
what became euphemistically known as “the

00:16:34.369 --> 00:16:38.889
Services Group”, a gang of child sex slaves
the dictator could use whenever he wanted.

00:16:38.889 --> 00:16:41.259
Finally, there are the tales of the freezers.

00:16:41.259 --> 00:16:46.389
After the 2011 revolution, a large group of
ice rooms were discovered below Gaddafi’s

00:16:46.389 --> 00:16:47.389
palace.

00:16:47.389 --> 00:16:50.930
Inside each one lay some rival who’d been
killed on the Mad Dog’s orders.

00:16:50.930 --> 00:16:53.310
Some had been there for over 25 years.

00:16:53.310 --> 00:16:57.579
It’s said Gaddafi would often come down
here in the night, and touch their frozen

00:16:57.579 --> 00:17:01.129
corpses, to be reminded again of his omnipotence.

00:17:01.129 --> 00:17:05.689
Of his God-like power over life and death.

00:17:05.689 --> 00:17:09.850
These tales don’t even begin to scratch
the surface of the regime’s horrors in the

00:17:09.850 --> 00:17:10.850
‘90s.

00:17:10.850 --> 00:17:15.480
There’s the day in 1996 when guards walked
into Abu Salim political prison in Tripoli,

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:19.350
dropped grenades into the cells, and killed
1,270 men.

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:23.490
Or the extreme methods used to crackdown on
those who began a tribal rebellion against

00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:25.230
his rule in 1993.

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:30.179
Or the way the sanctions bit so hard that
speculating in water became punishable by

00:17:30.179 --> 00:17:31.179
death.

00:17:31.179 --> 00:17:36.810
All in all, Libya in the 1990s was as great
a horror show as during the Italian occupation.

00:17:36.810 --> 00:17:38.390
Perhaps greater.

00:17:38.390 --> 00:17:41.530
Yet Gaddafi still retained one unlikely friend.

00:17:41.530 --> 00:17:45.740
Remember that mention of Nelson Mandela a
few moments ago?

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:50.770
Now free, Mandela spent most of the ‘90s
trying to talk his onetime sponsor into turning

00:17:50.770 --> 00:17:52.260
over the Lockerbie bombers.

00:17:52.260 --> 00:17:56.750
In 1999, the South African statesman managed
to extract both a promise to do just that

00:17:56.750 --> 00:18:01.330
from Gaddafi, and an apology for the murder
of Yvonne Fletcher back in 1984.

00:18:01.330 --> 00:18:06.020
But it would be a world-changing event that
finally brought Gaddafi in from the cold.

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:10.480
In the aftermath of 9/11, he became the first
Arab leader to offer both condolences and

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:13.230
practical military support to the USA.

00:18:13.230 --> 00:18:19.510
Suddenly, in the crazy new War on Terror era,
Mad Dog Gaddafi didn’t seem such a bad guy

00:18:19.510 --> 00:18:20.710
after all.

00:18:20.710 --> 00:18:26.850
The Last Decade – The Statesman, and the
Revolution

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:31.470
For the relatives of Gaddafi’s many victims,
the Noughties were like falling through the

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:32.690
Looking Glass.

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:37.049
After Libya paid reparations to the Lockerbie
families, the UN lifted its sanctions.

00:18:37.049 --> 00:18:42.760
Then, in 2004, Gaddafi suddenly announced
Libya was giving up all its chemical and biological

00:18:42.760 --> 00:18:43.760
weapons.

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:47.700
As a reward, both the George Bush and Tony
Blair governments began encouraging business

00:18:47.700 --> 00:18:49.179
investment in Libya.

00:18:49.179 --> 00:18:53.950
The UK’s MI6 even kidnapped the families
of two Libyan dissidents from Bangkok and

00:18:53.950 --> 00:18:57.270
handed them over to Tripoli with a note saying
“thank you”.

00:18:57.270 --> 00:19:01.539
This new, international phase for Gaddafi
was marked by him formally turning his back

00:19:01.539 --> 00:19:03.100
on the Arab world.

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:06.400
In a public speech, he begged God to keep
the Arabs out of Libya.

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:10.760
It seemed that, if he couldn’t be the Nasser
of the Arabs, then Gaddafi would settle for

00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:13.500
being the Nasser of another people.

00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:18.149
From the mid-2000s on, Gaddafi began laying
the ground for his final, quixotic dream:

00:19:18.149 --> 00:19:20.390
a United States of Africa.

00:19:20.390 --> 00:19:25.590
This Africa would have one passport, one external
border, one army… and one ruler.

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:28.690
Care to guess who he thought was the man for
the job?

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:34.440
Most African leaders unsurprisingly dismissed
the Mad Dog’s latest fantasy, but not all.

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:39.160
In 2008, Gaddafi managed to convince over
200 African kings and tribal chiefs to crown

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:42.010
him the “King of Kings” of all of Africa.

00:19:42.010 --> 00:19:47.210
It was classic Gaddafi pageantry, but it did
have immediate consequences.

00:19:47.210 --> 00:19:51.290
As a show of good will, he threw Libya’s
borders open to all Africans.

00:19:51.290 --> 00:19:55.620
The result was four and a half million Arab
and Muslim Libyans resentfully being forced

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:59.830
to share their country with a million and
a half Christian Africans.

00:19:59.830 --> 00:20:05.159
While the USAfrica never went anywhere, the
decade ended with Libya on its best international

00:20:05.159 --> 00:20:06.159
footing since 1969.

00:20:06.159 --> 00:20:12.130
In 2009, Gaddafi even managed to use his diplomatic
clout to have the Lockerbie bomber freed on

00:20:12.130 --> 00:20:13.669
compassionate grounds.

00:20:13.669 --> 00:20:16.350
All in all, things were looking up for the
monster of the Maghreb.

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:19.030
He was the longest-serving non-royal leader
on Earth.

00:20:19.030 --> 00:20:21.179
He had friends in Europe and America.

00:20:21.179 --> 00:20:22.830
The petro-money was pouring in.

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:25.210
Gaddafi in 2010 looked unstoppable.

00:20:25.210 --> 00:20:31.070
And he might have been, were it not for an
anonymous Tunisian street vendor named Mohamed

00:20:31.070 --> 00:20:32.230
Bouazizi.

00:20:32.230 --> 00:20:38.299
On December 17, 2010, Bouazizi set himself
on fire to protest his harassment by Tunisian

00:20:38.299 --> 00:20:39.320
authorities.

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:44.240
He died of his wounds a couple of weeks later,
but his suicide led to large-scale protests

00:20:44.240 --> 00:20:47.279
that forced Tunisia’s autocratic ruler from
power.

00:20:47.279 --> 00:20:51.710
The Arab Spring was here, and it was about
to hit Libya like a hurricane.

00:20:51.710 --> 00:20:54.130
If you’re of a certain age, you may remember
what happened next.

00:20:54.130 --> 00:20:58.870
Gaddafi was so sure his people loved him that
he was caught completely on the backfoot when

00:20:58.870 --> 00:21:02.360
the country exploded in revolt on February
15, 2011.

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:05.440
Benghazi fell, then the rest of the east.

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:09.289
Finally aware of what was happening, Gaddafi
dropped the mask of moderation he’d worn

00:21:09.289 --> 00:21:10.610
since 9/11.

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:14.620
The Mad Dog came roaring back, and he was
hungry for blood.

00:21:14.620 --> 00:21:18.399
As the world watched on, the Libyan Army began
rolling towards Benghazi.

00:21:18.399 --> 00:21:19.740
Those inside were trapped.

00:21:19.740 --> 00:21:22.590
Fearing an impending massacre, NATO stepped
in.

00:21:22.590 --> 00:21:26.419
A no-fly zone quickly turned into bombing
runs against Gaddafi’s units.

00:21:26.419 --> 00:21:31.560
With Western airpower now on their side, the
rebels broke out of Benghazi and pushed West.

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:34.480
In no time at all, Gaddafi’s forces were
in retreat.

00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:39.710
The resulting civil war lasted 8 months, and
killed somewhere in the region of 20,000 people.

00:21:39.710 --> 00:21:43.590
In its end days, the world witnessed all of
Gaddafi’s worst impulses.

00:21:43.590 --> 00:21:46.930
He ordered the starvation of Misurata in a
horrifying siege.

00:21:46.930 --> 00:21:48.970
His jailers tortured prisoners to death.

00:21:48.970 --> 00:21:51.740
His soldiers were given orders to rape civilians.

00:21:51.740 --> 00:21:55.029
Yet Gaddafi simply couldn’t counter NATO’s
airpower.

00:21:55.029 --> 00:21:59.110
By the end of summer, he was in hiding in
Sirte – the one time fishing village he’d

00:21:59.110 --> 00:22:05.240
been born outside, now transformed by four
decades of his patronage into a bustling city.

00:22:05.240 --> 00:22:09.480
On October 20, 2011, rebel fighters finally
overran its defenses.

00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:14.269
Gaddafi was found hiding in a storm drain,
his flamboyant clothes soaked with blood and

00:22:14.269 --> 00:22:17.780
excrement, surrounded by rubbish and cowering
like a rat.

00:22:17.780 --> 00:22:20.820
Sadly, in Libya, cruelty still remained the
currency.

00:22:20.820 --> 00:22:23.159
As Gaddafi whimpered “what’s this, my
sons?

00:22:23.159 --> 00:22:26.730
What are you doing?” he was surrounded by
a jeering crowd.

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:30.450
One soldier took his bayonet and sodomized
the dictator with it, slicing his insides

00:22:30.450 --> 00:22:31.450
open.

00:22:31.450 --> 00:22:35.100
As phones were waved to record his last moments,
gunshots rang out.

00:22:35.100 --> 00:22:36.100
The crowd panicked.

00:22:36.100 --> 00:22:37.179
The images went blurry.

00:22:37.179 --> 00:22:42.750
When they finally refocused, Gaddafi was dead,
nothing more than a bloodied corpse in the

00:22:42.750 --> 00:22:43.750
back of a truck.

00:22:43.750 --> 00:22:47.860
The first and last King of All Africa was
no more.

00:22:47.860 --> 00:22:52.900
Today, some seven years after Gaddafi’s
unceremonious execution, Libya is a mess of

00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:55.419
tribal conflict and deadly instability.

00:22:55.419 --> 00:22:59.559
There are at least five groups in control
of swathes of the country, on top of countless

00:22:59.559 --> 00:23:00.559
militia.

00:23:00.559 --> 00:23:06.019
Over 10,000 have died since the post-Gaddafi
settlement collapsed in 2014, making it one

00:23:06.019 --> 00:23:08.210
of the worst wars currently raging.

00:23:08.210 --> 00:23:11.870
In the end, perhaps all Gaddafi really did
was hold his country back.

00:23:11.870 --> 00:23:16.260
For a nation forged in the cruelty of the
Italian occupation, the cruelty of the Gaddafi

00:23:16.260 --> 00:23:20.820
regime led only naturally to the cruelty of
today’s conflict.

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:24.289
Maybe under a different leader Libya could
have been something more.

00:23:24.289 --> 00:23:40.320
As with so much in history, we will likely
never know.

